Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.120
I'm fascinated with chess in movies

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.040
and not just because I played chess in middle school

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.380
and competitively in high school

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.200
and college

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.480
chess is so interesting because of how much meaning it can have in film

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.140
whether it's through analogy, metaphor, or even a plot device

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:19.220
filmmakers love using the game to add to their stories in a way no other game can

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.140
so, let's play

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.420
1: I'll teach y'all if y'all wanna learn 2: c'mon man nah c'mon we're right in the middle of a game

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.460
the most basic use of chess is for an analogy

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.480
it's usually used to associate something with patience, or precision

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:33.160
boxing's a chess game, y'know? gotta pick my punches to take 'em down

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.540
Independance Day sets up the main character as a chess player

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.220
and he references the game throughout the film

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:38.740
ah it's like in chess

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.840
first you strategically position the pieces,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.380
then when the timings right, you strike

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.960
the next step is to use chess to show somebody as smart

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.140
and this is where filmmakers get clever

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.400
in 2001: A Space Odyssey, Hal's intelligence is established in his ability to beat humans in chess

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.000
the importance of this scene is somewhat lost on modern movie-goers

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.540
because computers have been beating the best chess players for the last twenty years

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:03.640
but in 1968, it showed that Hal was not only intelligent, but very powerful

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.980
if you look at the scene more closely, it also foreshadows an important plot point

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.820
when this supercomputer tells the human the better choice

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.860
HAL: I'm sorry Frank, I think you missed it

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.080
Queen to Bishop 3, Bishop takes Queen

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.000
Knight takes bishop, mate

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.280
through chess we see the computer disagreeing with the human,

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:27.460
and making a better move for him, showing very subtly that the computer can conflict with and outsmart him.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.080
this foreshadows the plot quite nicely

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.540
I'm sorry Dave, I'm afraid I can't do that

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.300
Hal actually makes a mistake while talking about the game

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.820
his move suggestion, queen to bishop 3, is impossible in this position

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.080
so Hal does have the forced checkmate, but is declaring it incorrectly

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:44.580
now you could say this was either a mistake on Kubrick's part,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:49.300
or, more interestingly, Kubrick is very subtly foreshadowing Hal's eventual breakdown

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.360
the duel between Hal and Frank Pool brings me to my third point

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.360
chess works nicely to represent a battle between two people

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.940
this can be as discreet or symbolic as you like

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:02.300
in Sherlock Holmes, the montage of the chess game with actual conflict makes the idea pretty obvious

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.680
it's a battle of wits. but the events of the game almost always stand for something more

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:10.620
chess is a very versatile game; Pixar made a short with the entire plot revolving around a chess battle

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.620
for example, it's popular to use chess to show a person's sacrifice

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.540
maybe the most literal instance of this is Harry Potter

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.080
Knight to H3

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:25.040
Sherlock goes for a queen sacrifice in his game, directly mirroring Sherlock's plan to stop Moriarity

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.780
The winning strategy sometimes necessitates sacrifice

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.980
to go one step further, you can even let the chess pieces themselves serve as metaphors for what's happening in the movie

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:38.700
Magneto fights with the powerful rook and knight pieces, but Professor X wins by sacrificing his powerful pieces, and

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.240
using his pawns to checkmate

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:41.980
these moves reflect each player's worldview

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:45.700
Magneto's strong pieces represent him fighting with mutants against the humans,

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.320
while Professor X fights with the pawns, showing his desire to work with and trust the humans

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.500
this metaphor serves the movie nicely, because in chess a pawn can actually mutate into a knight or rook

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.440
and this metaphor is further supported when humans are referred to as pawns throughout the X-Men movies

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.780
in chess, the pawns go first

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.800
I think chess metaphors are most powerful when they add to the story

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.960
in The People VS OJ Simpson, Shapiro and Johnny Carson both have chess sets in their offices during

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:17.140
the episode where they decide to make their defense arguments center around race

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:19.480
in one shot, the chess set is right in the foreground

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.500
here we see an obvious choice to have this chess set as the focal point in the shot

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:24.920
and  I think it may mean something

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:30.420
of course, I hesitate to read too far into this, but it's worth nothing that chess is a battle between black and white pieces

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:34.820
and in the context of this scene, about black vs white people, that makes for a powerful visual

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:38.260
and adds to the idea that this trial was a battle centering on race

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.980
maybe the perfect summation of all these techniques comes from this chess scene in the tv show The Wire

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.940
D essentially uses chess to explain the drug trade

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.760
the rooks are the stash, the queen is the muscle, and the king is top dog

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:52.380
they even go so far as to naming the pieces after actual people in their gang

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:54.700
But he ain't got no hussle

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.920
but the rest of these motherfuckers on the team, they got his back

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.900
and they run so deep, he really ain't gotta do shit

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.320
like ya uncle

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:03.760
yeah, like my uncle

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:08.020
the pawns are described as the soldiers, and in reality, these kids are the soldiers in their gang

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:10.160
and so they naturally identify with the pawns

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.460
what's interesting is what Vaulus's question is after he learns about the panws

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.420
they like the front lines, they be out in the field

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.360
so how do you get to be the king?

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:19.820
it ain't like that

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.460
see, the king, stay the king

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.980
aight? everything stay who he is

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.380
as the pawns they always have to serve the king no matter what

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.180
even if they get to the other side of the board

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.820
if you catch, the other dude's king

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.840
and trap it, then you win

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:36.800
alright but if I make it to the end

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.020
I'm top dog

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.200
nah yo it ain't like that

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:45.320
through chess we learn about the drug trade, and how its hierarchy is as strict as the rules of a board game

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:49.080
no matter what, these characters are trapped in their roles with no way to escape

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.700
they get sacrificed for the king, and no matter how they play, they never get to be in charge

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:57.100
the pawns, man, in the game, they get capped quick

00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:59.220
they be out the game early

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.380
unless they some smart ass pawns

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.520
in movies, the use of most games is one dimensional

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.020
bingo is there to establich you're in a retirement home

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:12.320
if you're playing Dungeons and Dragons, the characters are stereotyped as nerdy

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.880
but with chess, there's almost always more to analyze

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.100
so get out there and make a move

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:17.080
where's His Majesty?

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.260
oh, sir, the king is playing chess

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:20.440
Chess?

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:22.420
I hate chess!

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:24.400
